The Status Reports App provides a centralized and easy way for project and delivery leaders to generate professional, consistent status reports. It eliminates the need for manual formatting, ensures accuracy, and enables seamless sharing across leadership teams.
Visibility at a Glance – Access clear and current reports across programs, projects, and initiatives.
	•	Consistency – Standardized format makes progress easy to compare and track.
	•	Efficiency – Reduce time spent building reports; focus on outcomes and decisions.
	•	Seamless Sharing – Reports are generated and shared via link, ensuring leadership has the latest information.
	•	Always Up to Date – Reports can be updated anytime by editing project details.

How It Works
	1.	Create Project – Enter project name, status, actions, and milestones.
	2.	Generate Report – The app automatically formats a clean status report.
	3.	Release & Share – A report link is created for sharing with leadership.
	4.	Update – Edit project data to refresh the report on the required cadence.
•	Frontend (UI) – Custom web interface for creating and editing projects.
	•	Backend Service – Handles business logic, generates reports, and manages project state.
	•	Database – Stores project metadata, actions, milestones, and report versions.
	•	Report Generator – Converts stored project data into a consistent, shareable report format.

Data Model (Simplified)
	•	Project
	•	Project ID
	•	Project Name
	•	Owner / Team
	•	Current Status (RAG: Red/Amber/Green)
	•	Key Actions / Risks
	•	Milestones
	•	Report
	•	Report ID
	•	Linked Project ID
	•	Generated Date
	•	Release Version
	•	Shared Link

Example Use Cases
	•	Weekly delivery status updates
	•	Steering committee reporting
	•	Tracking program milestones and risks
	•	Creating a “single source of truth” for ongoing initiatives

Tech Stack Overview
	1.	Frontend (React App)
	•	Source code lives in /src/webapp.
	•	It’s a simple single-page application (SPA) without heavy complexity.
	•	Handles only UI rendering, while authentication and data handling are delegated to the backend.
	2.	Backend (Go Application)
	•	Source code lives in /src/backend.
	•	Responsible for authentication, connectivity requests, and APIs.
	•	Built using Go and deployed as an AWS Lambda function.
	3.	Build & Deployment
	•	React build process:
	•	At deployment time, React code is compiled into static assets (HTML, CSS, JS) and placed into /src/backend/dist.
	•	Go build process:
	•	The Go backend is compiled into an executable binary located at /src/backend/backend.
	•	Together, this setup ensures the Lambda function can serve both:
	•	Static frontend assets (the React SPA).
	•	Backend APIs for authentication and connectivity.
	4.	Hosting
	•	The Go Lambda acts as the single entry point:
	•	Serves the React SPA (static content).
	•	Exposes backend routes for authentication, API calls, and data requests.

Step-by-step:
	1.	A user opens the app in the browser.
	2.	The Go Lambda serves the compiled React static files (/dist).
	3.	The React SPA is loaded on the client side.
	4.	For any backend needs (e.g., login, data fetch), the React app makes API calls back to the Go Lambda.
	5.	The Go Lambda processes requests and interacts with AWS services or external APIs.
	6.	Results are returned as JSON responses to the frontend, which updates the UI.

•	Single deployment unit: both frontend and backend are packaged together.
	•	Lightweight SPA with minimal complexity.
	•	Scalable because Go Lambda can handle both static hosting and backend APIs.
	•	Secure as all sensitive logic (auth, connectivity) stays in the backend.
